{
  "message": "You’re very welcome! I’m glad to hear your connection is now excellent and fast. If you ever need help again, feel free to reach out. Have a great day and enjoy your improved mobile data!"
}

{
  "message": "Thank you, John. I have located your account using your phone number. To help you with your MMS (picture message) issue, I’ll guide you through a few simple steps. First, could you please check if you are able to make regular calls and use mobile data (for example, browsing the web) on your phone? This will help us determine if the issue is specific to MMS or related to your overall service."
}

{
  "message": "No problem, John! I’ll walk you through a simple way to check if your mobile data is working. Please look at the top of your phone’s screen (the status bar) and see if you notice any of these icons: 4G, LTE, 5G, H+, or E. These usually mean mobile data is available. If you’re not sure, I can guide you to check your phone’s status more directly. Would you like instructions on how to check your phone’s status bar for these icons, or would you prefer to try using your phone’s web browser to see if you can load a website?"
}

{
  "message": "The airplane icon means that Airplane Mode is turned on, which disables all wireless connections, including mobile data and MMS. To fix this, please turn off Airplane Mode on your phone. You can usually do this by swiping down from the top of your screen to open the quick settings panel, then tapping the airplane icon so it is no longer highlighted. Once you’ve turned off Airplane Mode, let me know if you see any signal bars or data icons (like 4G, LTE, or 5G) at the top of your screen."
}

{
  "message": "Great job turning off Airplane Mode! Seeing signal bars is a good sign, but since your phone is showing '2G,' that network is usually too slow for sending picture messages (MMS). Let's try switching your phone to a faster network mode.\n\nPlease go to your phone's network settings and set your network mode to '4G/5G preferred' if possible. This will help your phone connect to a faster network, which is better for MMS. Let me know once you've done this, or if you need help finding the setting."
}

{
  "message": "That's perfect! Seeing '5G' with full bars means your phone is now connected to a fast network, which is ideal for sending picture messages (MMS). Please try sending a picture message now and let me know if it goes through successfully. If it still doesn't work, let me know and we'll continue troubleshooting together."
}

{
  "message": "I understand how frustrating this can be, John. Sometimes, the issue can be with your phone’s network settings, specifically something called the APN settings, which are needed for MMS to work. Let’s try resetting these settings to make sure everything is correct.

Please go to your phone’s network or mobile data settings and look for an option to reset your APN (Access Point Name) settings. After you reset the APN settings, you’ll need to restart (reboot) your phone for the changes to take effect.

Let me know once you’ve reset the APN settings and restarted your phone, or if you need help finding these options."
}

{
  "message": "No worries, John! Restarting (rebooting) your phone is completely safe and will not delete any of your photos, messages, or other personal data. It just turns your phone off and back on to refresh its settings. Please go ahead and restart your phone now. Once it’s back on, try sending a picture message again and let me know if it works."
}

{
  "message": "Thank you for your patience, John. Since you've reset your APN settings, restarted your phone, and are connected to 5G, let's check one more thing that can sometimes interfere with picture messages: Wi-Fi Calling. If Wi-Fi Calling is turned on, it can sometimes prevent MMS from working properly.\n\nPlease check your phone's settings to see if Wi-Fi Calling is enabled. If it is, please turn it off and then try sending a picture message again. Let me know if you need help finding this setting or if turning off Wi-Fi Calling helps."
}

{
  "message": "Thank you for checking that, John. Since Wi-Fi Calling is off and we've tried the main steps, the next thing to check is whether your messaging app has the necessary permissions to send and receive picture messages. Sometimes, if the app doesn't have permission to access your phone's storage or SMS, MMS won't work.\n\nPlease go to your phone's settings, find your messaging app, and make sure it has permission to access both 'Storage' and 'SMS.' If you need help finding these settings, let me know what kind of phone you have (Android or iPhone), and I can guide you step by step."
}

